
A user component with an empty body is dead code, so the optimizer drops the
declaration while the call site's node survives pointing at it. android used
to fall through to the same generic emitter the stdlib components used, which
picked a prop off the node and drew it as text: `blank(value="hello")`
rendered `Text("hello")` for a component whose body says to draw nothing.
There is no generic emitter any more, and an eliminated user component draws
what its body said: nothing, and not a placeholder either. A component that
does have a body still renders it.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `hello` -- the eliminated component's prop reaches nothing; no emitter is left to draw it as text
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `unknown` -- an empty body emits nothing at all, not a placeholder naming the dropped component
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component blank(value string) node {
}

component shown(caption string) node {
    vbox {
        text(value=caption)
    }
}

component main node {
    blank(value="hello")
    shown(caption="x")
}

window {
    main
}
